    "turn_type_label": "Substantive",
    "conversation_move_label": "Correction / Challenge"
  },
  {
    "episode_id": 66007,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/6/6/66007.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 27,
    "turn_text": "I just wonder where conservatism has gone, because if you consider yourself a conservative in the tradition of Edmund Burke, then what is sacred for a nation? It's our traditions and our institutions. And one can think of nothing more sacred than the United States Capitol. I know that there were some commentators, conservative commentators saying, why is the left goal was invoking religious imagery to speak about the Capitol? Well, they should read Lincoln because Lincoln calls these our national religion. in the symbols, the temples of democracy, and so the attack on the Capitol. It wasn't an attack with members of Congress. It wasn't an attack on a party. It was an attack on our national way of life on everything. I mean, you grow up idolizing most people that the Capitol and American democracy. And it's been It's sad to me to see that there hasn't been more Republican voices saying the Capitol building in the American democracy is the greatest tribute to American democracy in the history of the world. We should be so proud of this building. That is a building of American exceptionalism. And anyone who attacks it or contributes to the attack of it should be held accountable.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_00",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
