    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/6/6/66007.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 36,
    "turn_text": "I'm for it. A lot of members are for it. The question always becomes what do you have in its place? And the problem is that that's where the disagreement always comes. I would say why don't we have a limited AUMF in its place and ask the president to come. Here's what I've never understood. If the president really has a case for attack and force, I don't think Congress is going to be the obstacle. So if President Biden had to be forced to come to the Congress to say the Iranian militia has hit our troops, I want authorization for a limited strike back, and we can debate the merits of it. And there are reasons probably not to escalate. But I'll tell you what, I think Congress actually would approve that. I'm not saying how I would have voted. So the question, the point is, if you show me examples of the president coming for authorization to Congress where Congress is not approving the authorizations and that this is having repeatedly, then maybe I would say, okay, you know, we have a challenge. My view is we haven't even tried it. And I for the life of me don't understand why presidents don't just come to Congress for authorization. When more often than not, they probably received that",
